Zheng20231907,Distributed Dispatch of Integrated Electricity-Heat Systems With Variable Mass Flow,family,8,4,pdf,7,no,no,"To devise a practical solution, we investigate a sur- rogate model to describe the heat dynamics. Then (P1-H) is approximated as a continuous nonlinear program by replacing the node method equations with the surrogate thermal dynamic equations, which can be readily solved by commercial solvers. Surrogate Thermal Dynamic Model Basically, the node method is developed by discretizing the solution of the following partial differential equation [ 28] that describes the thermal dynamics: cρA b ∂τ ∂t + cm∂τ ∂x + λb ( τ− τA ) = 0, ∀b (34) where x measures the distance from the starting end of pipeline b and τA denotes the ambient temperature."
Zheng20231907,Distributed Dispatch of Integrated Electricity-Heat Systems With Variable Mass Flow,family,9,4,pdf,7,no,no,"Then (P1-H) is approximated as a continuous nonlinear program by replacing the node method equations with the surrogate thermal dynamic equations, which can be readily solved by commercial solvers. Surrogate Thermal Dynamic Model Basically, the node method is developed by discretizing the solution of the following partial differential equation [ 28] that describes the thermal dynamics: cρA b ∂τ ∂t + cm∂τ ∂x + λb ( τ− τA ) = 0, ∀b (34) where x measures the distance from the starting end of pipeline b and τA denotes the ambient temperature. Note that ( 34)i s not discretized yet and therefore there are no subscripts b and t in the mass ﬂow temperature variable τ and the mass ﬂow variable m."
Zheng20231907,Distributed Dispatch of Integrated Electricity-Heat Systems With Variable Mass Flow,family,10,4,pdf,7,no,no,"Surrogate Thermal Dynamic Model Basically, the node method is developed by discretizing the solution of the following partial differential equation [ 28] that describes the thermal dynamics: cρA b ∂τ ∂t + cm∂τ ∂x + λb ( τ− τA ) = 0, ∀b (34) where x measures the distance from the starting end of pipeline b and τA denotes the ambient temperature. Note that ( 34)i s not discretized yet and therefore there are no subscripts b and t in the mass ﬂow temperature variable τ and the mass ﬂow variable m. To remove the non-elementary function ( 2) and the compli- cating dynamic vector ξ, a surrogate thermal dynamic model is derived here by approximating the partial derivatives in (34) with a ﬁrst-order difference: ⎧ ⎪⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎨ ⎪⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎩ cρA b [ 0.5 ( τPS,in b,t + τPS,out b,t ) − 0.5 ( τPS,in b,t−1 + τPS,out b,t−1 )] + cm ( τPS,out b,t − τPS,in b,t ) /Lb + λb [ 0.5 ( τPS,in b,t + τPS,out b,t ) − τA ] = 0,∀b, t cρAb [ 0.5 ( τPR,in b,t + τPR,out b,t ) − 0.5 ( τPR,in b,t−1 + τPR,out b,t−1 )] + cm ( τPR,out b,t − τPR,in b,t ) /Lb + λb [ 0.5 ( τPR,in b,t + τPR,out b,t ) − τA ] = 0,∀b, t (35) where we approximate ∂τ/∂t by referring to the average tem- perature of pipeline b, and τPS,in b,t /τPS,out b,t and τPR,in b,t /τPR,out b,t denote mass ﬂow temperature at the inlet/outlet of pipeline b at period t, respectively."
