De Castro20247710,Multi-Fidelity Reinforcement Learning for Minimum Energy Trajectory Planning,pattern,6,9,pdf,2,no,no,"These polynomials map time to position and yaw, i.e. , p(t) = pr(t)⊺ pψ(t)⊺ (pr(t) ∈ C 4, pψ(t) ∈ C 2), and each polynomial segment connects two adjacent waypoints. S1 denotes the circular angle space, and Cn is the differentiability class where its n-th order derivatives exist and are continuous. The coefficients of the polynomial trajectory are deter- mined by solving the following optimization problem: minimize p=[pr,pψ],x∈Rm ≥0 σ (x, p) subject to p(0) = ˜p0, p Pi j=1xj  = ˜pi, i = 1, . . . , m, p ∈ P (1) where σ(x, p) = Z Pm i=1xi 0 d4pr d4t 2 +  d2pψ d2t 2 dt. (2) P represents the set of dynamically feasible trajectories, i.e., trajectories that require admissible motor speed commands. xi, the pivotal optimization variable, denotes the time al- location between two consecutive waypoints ˜pi−1 and ˜pi (x = x1 · · · xm  ), and is exclusively determined through non-linear optimization."
De Castro20247710,Multi-Fidelity Reinforcement Learning for Minimum Energy Trajectory Planning,pattern,7,8,pdf,2,yes,no,"This method can be computationally expen- sive, depending on the fe. This optimization method serves as the baseline for comparing the energy consumption against our resulting trained policy. Multi-fidelity Gaussian Process In this paper, we employ the Multi-fidelity Gaussian Process (MFGP) to efficiently model the energy function based on sparse multi-fidelity evaluations."
De Castro20247710,Multi-Fidelity Reinforcement Learning for Minimum Energy Trajectory Planning,pattern,8,8,pdf,3,yes,no,"Accurate energy consumption estima- tion necessitates complex simulations, leading to extended evaluation times. To reduce the number of necessary energy consumption estimations for training, we create a proxy model for energy estimation, denoted ˆfe, using a Gaus- sian process. We employ a multi-fidelity kernel within the Gaussian process, complemented by a hierarchical evaluation method, to enhance the efficiency of model construction."
De Castro20247710,Multi-Fidelity Reinforcement Learning for Minimum Energy Trajectory Planning,pattern,9,8,pdf,3,yes,no,"To reduce the number of necessary energy consumption estimations for training, we create a proxy model for energy estimation, denoted ˆfe, using a Gaus- sian process. We employ a multi-fidelity kernel within the Gaussian process, complemented by a hierarchical evaluation method, to enhance the efficiency of model construction. Subsequently, the planning policy is trained with RL, aiming to minimize energy consumption estimates obtained from the Gaussian process model."
De Castro20247710,Multi-Fidelity Reinforcement Learning for Minimum Energy Trajectory Planning,pattern,10,8,pdf,4,yes,yes,"Energy Prediction Model We introduce an MFGP-based energy prediction model as a surrogate for the two forward-loop simulations discussed in the previous section. Integrating these simulations directly into trajectory optimization presents significant challenges due to the high computational demands and difficulty in optimization formulation. To address this, we develop an MFGP-based model capable of predicting the high-fidelity SOC (SOC hf) for trajectories derived from sequences of waypoints and time allocations."
Hu2024,Probabilistic net load forecasting based on transformer network and Gaussian process-enabled residual modeling learning method,doe,1,8,pdf,2,yes,no,The main contributions of this article are summarized as follows. 1) This paper proposes a probabilistic net load forecasting method based on a Transformer network and Gaussian process-enabled re - sidual modeling learning (RML) method. The Transformer network is first applied to discover the complex structure in net load utilizing abundant historical data.
Hu2024,Probabilistic net load forecasting based on transformer network and Gaussian process-enabled residual modeling learning method,doe,2,8,pdf,2,yes,no,"1) This paper proposes a probabilistic net load forecasting method based on a Transformer network and Gaussian process-enabled re - sidual modeling learning (RML) method. The Transformer network is first applied to discover the complex structure in net load utilizing abundant historical data. Then, the forecasting errors are modeled by a Gaussian process (GP) to capture the prediction behavior of the pre- trained Transformer network."
