 ψ1 and ψ2 are two control parameters, which can be presented as ⎧ ⎪⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎨ ⎪⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎪ ⎩ ψ 1 = − ΥG χ1sgn(g1(Qr 1,G ) − g1(Qr 2,G )) (Qr 1,G − Qr 2,G )2 √∑ j ∈n 1 (Qr 1,G − Qr 2,G )2 ψ2 = − ΥG χ2sgn(g2(Qr 3,G ) − g2(Qr 4,G )) (Qr 3,G − Qr 4,G )2 √∑ j ∈n 1 (Qr 3,G − Qr 4,G )2 ΥG =Υ 0[(Gmax − G + 1)/Gmax ]p (48) where ΥG , χi , ψ1, and ψ2 are control parameters, Gmax denotes the maximum generation number. By replacing the classic mutation operator with the abovementioned gradient descent operator, the convergence speed can be improved."
Yang2024__2,Data-driven robust optimization scheduling for microgrid day-ahead to intra-day operations based on renewable energy interval prediction,pattern,1,15,pdf,8,no,no,"Through this process, the DQN algorithm can train the agent stably, enabling it to learn the optimal strategy in the given environment. The DQN algorithm training process mainly includes the following four components: 1) State-action value function The state-action value function Q ( s t , a t ; ω ) evaluates the action taken in the current state. In the DQN algorithm, the update rule for the state- action value function is as follows: Q t + 1 ( s t , a t ; ω ) = Q t ( s t , a ; ω ) + α [ r t + γ max a ʹ Q ʹ ( s t , a ʹ ; ω ʹ ) ␀ Q t ( s t , a ; ω ) ] (41) In the equation, Q ( s t , a t ; ω ) represents the state-action value function, Q ʹ ( s t + 1 , a ʹ ; ω ʹ ) denotes the target value function, α is the learning rate, γ is the discount factor representing the discount of future Q at the current time, and a ʹ denotes the optimal action among all possible actions in state s t + 1 ."
Yang2024__2,Data-driven robust optimization scheduling for microgrid day-ahead to intra-day operations based on renewable energy interval prediction,pattern,2,15,pdf,8,no,no,"The DQN algorithm training process mainly includes the following four components: 1) State-action value function The state-action value function Q ( s t , a t ; ω ) evaluates the action taken in the current state. In the DQN algorithm, the update rule for the state- action value function is as follows: Q t + 1 ( s t , a t ; ω ) = Q t ( s t , a ; ω ) + α [ r t + γ max a ʹ Q ʹ ( s t , a ʹ ; ω ʹ ) ␀ Q t ( s t , a ; ω ) ] (41) In the equation, Q ( s t , a t ; ω ) represents the state-action value function, Q ʹ ( s t + 1 , a ʹ ; ω ʹ ) denotes the target value function, α is the learning rate, γ is the discount factor representing the discount of future Q at the current time, and a ʹ denotes the optimal action among all possible actions in state s t + 1 . 2) Cumulative Reward Function The cumulative reward function is used in reinforcement learning to evaluate the total reward that can be obtained by executing a certain Fig."
Yang2024__2,Data-driven robust optimization scheduling for microgrid day-ahead to intra-day operations based on renewable energy interval prediction,pattern,3,13,pdf,11,no,no,"proposed in this paper to construct the uncertainty set of renewable energy output for dispatching achieves better results. The dispatch plan formulated by this model can better cope with the uncertainty of renewable energy output, reducing the conservatism of dispatch de- cisions while ensuring the economic efficiency and robustness of system operation. Comparison and validation of uncertainty set representation methods To validate the effectiveness of the proposed method for representing new energy uncertainties based on interval probability uncertainty sets in robust optimization, the following methods are compared: Method 1 uses the adaptive robust optimization approach with μ -type uncertainty sets [ 43 ]; Method 2 uses the adaptive robust optimization approach with Γ -type uncertainty sets [ 44 ]; Method 3 uses the adaptive robust opti- mization approach with uncertainty sets generated by the Dirichlet process [ 45 ]; Method 4 uses the adaptive robust optimization approach with the novel uncertainty sets proposed in this paper."
Yang2024__2,Data-driven robust optimization scheduling for microgrid day-ahead to intra-day operations based on renewable energy interval prediction,pattern,4,13,pdf,11,no,no,"The dispatch plan formulated by this model can better cope with the uncertainty of renewable energy output, reducing the conservatism of dispatch de- cisions while ensuring the economic efficiency and robustness of system operation. Comparison and validation of uncertainty set representation methods To validate the effectiveness of the proposed method for representing new energy uncertainties based on interval probability uncertainty sets in robust optimization, the following methods are compared: Method 1 uses the adaptive robust optimization approach with μ -type uncertainty sets [ 43 ]; Method 2 uses the adaptive robust optimization approach with Γ -type uncertainty sets [ 44 ]; Method 3 uses the adaptive robust opti- mization approach with uncertainty sets generated by the Dirichlet process [ 45 ]; Method 4 uses the adaptive robust optimization approach with the novel uncertainty sets proposed in this paper. The composition of microgrid operating costs for different methods is shown in Table 7 ."
Yang2024__2,Data-driven robust optimization scheduling for microgrid day-ahead to intra-day operations based on renewable energy interval prediction,pattern,5,13,pdf,2,no,no,"Currently, solutions for robust optimization models commonly employ traditional intelligent algorithms or mathematical methods based on robust optimization. Dong et al. [28] proposes a constrained multi-objective transfer algorithm to solve multi-objective optimization problems with the operational costs and robustness of integrated energy systems as optimization goals. Although this method can balance mul- tiple optimization objectives, it has poor convergence and often fails to find the optimal value when dealing with programming problems involving complex constraints."
Yang2024__2,Data-driven robust optimization scheduling for microgrid day-ahead to intra-day operations based on renewable energy interval prediction,pattern,6,12,pdf,16,no,no,"The average solving time for a single intra-day short-term rolling optimization is only 0.51s. This is because the intra-day phase is based on multi-time scale theory, significantly shortening the time window length for single optimization and reducing the granularity of the optimization. Consequently, the time required for a single short time scale rolling optimization is also corre- spondingly reduced."
Yang2024__2,Data-driven robust optimization scheduling for microgrid day-ahead to intra-day operations based on renewable energy interval prediction,pattern,7,11,pdf,17,yes,no,"Furthermore, it further validates its practicality and reliability in practical application. Conclusions This paper proposes a robust optimization scheduling method for day-ahead and intra-day microgrid that integrates prediction, adjust- ment, and decision-making. The method uses an improved GPR algo- rithm to adaptively generate the interval prediction of renewable energy output under different confidence levels, linking it to the construction of the robust optimization uncertainty set."
Yang2024__2,Data-driven robust optimization scheduling for microgrid day-ahead to intra-day operations based on renewable energy interval prediction,pattern,8,11,pdf,17,yes,no,"Conclusions This paper proposes a robust optimization scheduling method for day-ahead and intra-day microgrid that integrates prediction, adjust- ment, and decision-making. The method uses an improved GPR algo- rithm to adaptively generate the interval prediction of renewable energy output under different confidence levels, linking it to the construction of the robust optimization uncertainty set. A MDQN algorithm is intro- duced to transform the robust model into a discrete Markov decision process for solution."
Yang2024__2,Data-driven robust optimization scheduling for microgrid day-ahead to intra-day operations based on renewable energy interval prediction,pattern,9,11,pdf,2,yes,no,"References [34,35] constructed stochastic optimization and robust optimization models to address uncertainties in new energy output and load demand during day-ahead to intra-day scheduling. However, the consideration of uncertainties in the above studies at the day-ahead stage tends to be overly risky or conservative: the former results in excessive costs for intra-day tracking of the day-ahead plan, sometimes failing to fully mitigate power fluctuations, while the latter may lead to total costs exceeding acceptable levels. To address the above problems, this paper proposes a two-stage day- ahead and intra-day microgrid scheduling framework integrating fore- casting, regulation, and decision-making."
Yang2024__2,Data-driven robust optimization scheduling for microgrid day-ahead to intra-day operations based on renewable energy interval prediction,pattern,10,10,pdf,11,no,no,"Comparison and validation of uncertainty set representation methods To validate the effectiveness of the proposed method for representing new energy uncertainties based on interval probability uncertainty sets in robust optimization, the following methods are compared: Method 1 uses the adaptive robust optimization approach with μ -type uncertainty sets [ 43 ]; Method 2 uses the adaptive robust optimization approach with Γ -type uncertainty sets [ 44 ]; Method 3 uses the adaptive robust opti- mization approach with uncertainty sets generated by the Dirichlet process [ 45 ]; Method 4 uses the adaptive robust optimization approach with the novel uncertainty sets proposed in this paper. The composition of microgrid operating costs for different methods is shown in Table 7 . From Tables 7 and it can be observed that, under the accuracy of the predicted data for each method, the operating costs for all four methods increase with the confidence level."
